{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Avenge the vengeance of the children of Israel from the Midianites.</b> And Moses changed and said: ״לָתֵת נִקְמַת ה׳ בְּמִדְיָן״ ('to give HaShem's vengeance upon Midian'). And there is further difficulty in what Rashi explained: even though Moses heard that his death was dependent on this matter, he did it with joy and did not delay. Whence does Rashi know to say so? Perhaps he was saddened about his death — and would on that account he not fulfill the word of HaShem? And who is like him in fulfilling the word of HaShem without delay? And as for what Rashi said, that ״וַיִּמָּסְרוּ״ ('they were delivered') teaches the praise of the leaders of Israel, etc., until they were delivered against their will — it is not explained in Scripture what was the compulsion that brought them to this." "And the explanation of the matter is that the Midianites committed two evils: one against Heaven, for they caused Israel to sin through idolatry and licentiousness; and one against Israel, for through their counsel they slew twenty-four thousand of Israel. And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, ״נְקֹם נִקְמַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ ('avenge the vengeance of the children of Israel') — for what they did to Me is foreborne and forgiven, for if the sins of man multiply, what can He, blessed be He, do? The essential vengeance is for what they did to Israel, for their evil was great. And when Moses heard that HaShem said to him ״אַחַר תֵּאָסֵף אֶל עַמֶּךָ״ ('afterward you shall be gathered to your people'), and Moses already knew the great love of Israel toward their leaders, he said in his heart: If I say to Israel the matters as they are, as they were spoken to me from the mouth of the Almighty — once Israel knows that my death depends on this vengeance, then Israel will say: just as the Holy One, blessed be He, forgave His portion, so too will we forgive them for what they did to us, and we will not seek vengeance so hastily, for it is in our hands to delay the time of vengeance until we desire. And Moses, since he was joyful to do the word of HaShem without delay, changed and said: ״לָתֵת נִקְמַת ה׳ בְּמִדְיָן״ — and Israel would reason: since the Holy One, blessed be He, seeks the vengeance for the sake of His honor, then who is there to authorize its delay? And through this they would be compelled to do it immediately. And it is of this compulsion that it says: ״וַיִּמָּסְרוּ״ — for since Moses said to them ״לָתֵת נִקְמַת ה׳ בְּמִדְיָן״, therefore they were compelled to act without delay. And from here is a clear proof that he did it with joy and did not delay, for it was in his hand to prolong the time of vengeance had he said to them the words of HaShem, blessed be He, as they were." "And the fact that it first writes ״מִדְיָנִים״ — in the plural — and afterward it says ״לָתֵת נִקְמַת ה׳ בְּמִדְיָן״ — in the singular — is because relative to Israel they are many: as though many did to Israel what they did. But relative to what they did to the Holy One, blessed be He, they are not counted as anything but a single man, for they are all reckoned as nothing and emptiness before Him, blessed be He." "And there are those who say ״בְּמִדְיָן״ means the prince of Midian above, in the manner that it says (Exodus 14:10) ״וְהִנֵּה מִצְרַיִם נֹסֵעַ״ ('and behold Egypt journeying') — which they interpreted (Midrash Rabbah, Exodus 22) as referring to the prince of Egypt's heavenly patron. And therefore it says ״לָתֵת נִקְמַת ה׳״, for the war above belongs to HaShem and not to man."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:39.526211924Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}